<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annaba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bône</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Annaba is a port city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, near the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a>. Known in medieval and early modern times as Bona or Bône, it was a target of European raids, including a sack by the Tuscan Knights of Saint Stephen in 1607. It became a French colonial port after 1832 and is now one of Algeria's largest cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annaba.jpg" alt="Annaba" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dan Sloan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annaba-building02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Order of Saint Stephen in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at Pisa, whose galleys fought the Ottomans and the Barbary corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked <b>Bona</b> on the Algerian coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
